  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Vigor Marine has $699M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Vigor Marine won 29 awards so far this fiscal year, with an average award size of $24.1M, and obligations are up 57.4% year over year. Vigor Marine received the bulk of FY2026 obligations from the Department of Defense, which obligated $664M. Vigor Marine’s work is concentrated in Ship Building and Repairing, which accounts for $698M in obligations, with small amounts recorded under Other Support Activities for Water Transportation and Steam and Air-Conditioning Supply. Historical annual obligations show a rise from $304M in FY21 to $699M in FY26, with intermediate levels of $353M in FY22, $309M in FY23, $448M in FY24, and $444M in FY25. Vigor Marine’s current-year totals are partial and reflect fiscal year to date obligations; the largest buyers and NAICS markets listed reflect FY2026 to date activity.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What drove the FY2026 increase in obligations?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $664M to Vigor Marine in FY2026 to date, which accounts for most of the increase. Vigor Marine’s Ship Building and Repairing market recorded $698M in obligations, aligning with the DoD buying pattern. Year-over-year obligations were up 57.4% for FY2026 to date. Vigor Marine’s average award size of $24.1M and the count of 29 awards show larger and concentrated awards this year."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where do Vigor Marine’s FY2026 obligations come from and what markets are involved?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense, the Department of Homeland Security, and the Department of Transportation are the top buying agencies, with $664M, $34M, and $1.5M respectively. Ship Building and Repairing is the dominant NAICS market with $698M in obligations. Vigor Marine has smaller obligations in Other Support Activities for Water Transportation at $1.5M and Steam and Air-Conditioning Supply at $2.8K. The award mix of 29 awards and an average award size of $24.1M reflects this concentration."
      }
    ]
  },
